Take $X=\mathbb{R}^2$, the subspace $M$ the $x$-axis, and $f(t,0)=t$ on $M$. With any of the norms $\|\cdot\|_1,\|\cdot\|_2,\|\cdot\|_\infty$ we have $|f(t,0)|=\|(t,0)\|$, so $\|f\|=1$ on $M$. The linear extensions are $F_c(x,y)=x+cy$, and the question is which have norm $1$ on all of $X$.

The norm of $F_c$ is the dual norm of the vector $(1,c)$, which is $\|\cdot\|_\infty$ for $(\mathbb{R}^2,\|\cdot\|_1)$, $\|\cdot\|_1$ for $(\mathbb{R}^2,\|\cdot\|_\infty)$ and $\|\cdot\|_2$ for the Euclidean norm. So:

- for $\|\cdot\|_1$, $\|F_c\|=\max(1,|c|)$, and every $c\in[-1,1]$ works;
- for $\|\cdot\|_\infty$, $\|F_c\|=1+|c|$, and only $c=0$ works;
- for $\|\cdot\|_2$, $\|F_c\|=\sqrt{1+c^2}$, and only $c=0$ works.

Geometrically, a norm-one extension is a line $F_c=1$ touching the unit ball at $(1,0)$ without cutting into it. The diamond of $\|\cdot\|_1$ has a corner at $(1,0)$, so there are many such supporting lines; the square and the disc have only one. Extensions always exist here, but need not be unique. The theorem says that existence survives in every dimension.
